        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: let agents remember across private conversations",
        "body": "Closes #99611\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nPersonal agents currently keep every private conversation isolated, so relevant context from another Telegram DM, WebUI session, TUI session, or other private surface is unavailable unless operators broaden session-tool visibility or assemble several advanced memory settings.\n\nThis leaves Owners without a safe, understandable choice between keeping conversations separate and allowing a trusted personal agent to remember relevant context across its private conversations.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds a default-off, per-agent `memorySearch.rememberAcrossConversations` setting. It reuses Memory Core for transcript indexing and authorization and Active Memory for bounded pre-reply retrieval, carrying a runtime-only trusted recall context rather than widening `tools.sessions.visibility` or merging transcripts.\n\nThe privacy boundary is fail-closed: recall is limited to the same agent's other recognized private conversations. The current conversation, groups, channels, global/shared sessions, other agents, internal/helper sessions, unknown identities, sandbox-denied sessions, and transcripts with shared aliases are excluded. Ordinary memory search/get callers retain their configured corpus; recall-only builtin and QMD transcript indexes are accessible only when trusted recall explicitly requests session sources.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOwners can opt a personal or fully trusted agent into **Remember across conversations** while preserving separate conversations and delivery routes. Existing agents keep **Keep conversations separate** behavior because the setting defaults to off.\n\nShared workspace Markdown memory is unchanged. Group and channel conversations remain isolated in both directions. `/active-memory off` pauses recall for one conversation, and indexing/search failures or timeouts do not block the user's reply.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `pnpm test <15 affected test files> -- --reporter=dot` — **906/906 passed** across config, agent plumbing, Memory Core, Active Memory, QMD, doctor, Plugin SDK, and QA Lab suites.\n- Builtin and QMD ordinary-search regressions were verified RED with the production source clamp temporarily removed, then GREEN after restoration.\n- `pnpm openclaw qa suite --provider-mode mock-openai --scenario remember-across-conversations --output-dir .artifacts/qa-e2e/remember-pr-final` — **1/1 passed** against a real Gateway child and qa-channel transport. The scenario proves positive private-to-private recall plus group/anchor exclusion, setting-off behavior, per-conversation pause, fresh-conversation independence, and unchanged `tools.sessions.visibility`.\n- `pnpm build` — passed.\n- `pnpm config:docs:check` — passed.\n- `pnpm plugin-sdk:api:check` — passed.\n- Exact local child form of `pnpm check:changed` — passed all production/test typechecks, lint shards, import-cycle checks, architecture/security/package guards, and extension contracts.\n- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` — passed.\n- Final Codex autoreview — no findings; patch correct (0.88).\n\nLive `openai/gpt-5.4 --fast` acceptance was not run locally because no approved API credential was available. The deterministic mock-provider scenario covers the complete Gateway/channel/privacy flow without provider variance.\n",
        "branch": "feat/remember-across-conversations",
